First Author: McInnis
Title: Chronic lung allograft dysfunction phenotype and prognosis by machine learning CT analysis
Source number: 35  ￼

Very short summary: This single-center retrospective study evaluated whether machine-learning CT texture analysis could phenotype chronic lung allograft dysfunction (CLAD) and predict graft failure after lung transplantation. Five key findings were: (1) 88 adult first double-lung transplant recipients with CLAD and qualifying CT scans were included; (2) the cohort comprised 57 BOS, 20 RAS/mixed, and 11 undefined/unclassified cases; (3) machine-learning pulmonary vessel volume (PVV) was the strongest discriminator of RAS/mixed phenotype with AUC 0.85; (4) machine-learning hyperlucent lung phenotyped BOS using inspiratory CT alone with AUC 0.76; and (5) both radiologist scores and machine-learning variables predicted graft failure independently of CLAD phenotype, with PVV showing the strongest prognostic association (HR 1.23, 95% CI 1.05–1.44; p = 0.01). Tables 1–4 and Figures 1–2 were used directly for this extraction.  ￼

