	•	Section 5 Comments/Quotes: The main diagnostic outcome was CLAD phenotyping. ROC analysis showed that GGOML, RETML, and especially PVVML identified the RAS/mixed phenotype, with PVV having the strongest AUC of 0.85. Using a 3% cut-point, PVV had sensitivity 90.0% and specificity 70.1% for RAS/mixed phenotyping. Machine-learning hyperlucent lung identified BOS on inspiratory CT alone with AUC 0.76. Prognostically, PVV was the strongest independent CT predictor of graft failure in multivariable Cox analysis (HR 1.23, 95% CI 1.05–1.44; p = 0.01). Accuracy, PPV, NPV, F1, calibration, and DCA were not reported.  ￼

Section 6: Quality Assessment (Risk of Bias / PROBAST)
	•	Participants (Selection Bias): High Risk | Quote/Reasoning: This was a single-center retrospective CLAD cohort, and from 211 CLAD patients only 88 were included after CT availability/quality and segmentation exclusions, which creates substantial selection risk.  ￼
	•	Predictors (Input Variable Bias): Low Risk | Quote/Reasoning: Predictors were clearly defined CT-derived machine-learning texture variables and blinded radiologist scores obtained near CLAD diagnosis.  ￼
	•	Outcome (Definition Bias): Low Risk | Quote/Reasoning: CLAD phenotype assignment was based on previously described methodology and considered the “gold standard,” and graft failure was explicitly defined as death or retransplantation.  ￼
	•	Analysis (Modeling Bias): Unclear | Quote/Reasoning: Strengths include blinded radiologist comparison, ROC analysis, interrater reliability assessment, and multivariable Cox models adjusted for key confounders. Uncertainty remains because this was a relatively small retrospective cohort without external validation.  ￼
	•	Applicability to Review Question: Low Concern | Quote/Reasoning: This article directly addresses AI-assisted CT phenotyping and prognosis in CLAD after lung transplantation and is highly relevant to the review question.  ￼

